{"op": "merge", "keep": {"id": "b8c5c85f-273e-4245-ac73-64ea13651980", "name": "LAS VEGAS SANDS CORPORATION"}, "lose": [{"id": "bc5b83a7-f40c-47ca-86f0-12e5919c41a7", "name": "LAS VEGAS SANDS CORP"}], "date": "2026-09-08", "note": "One casino company under two spellings of its own name, split by a hundredth of a point: the survivor already carried LAS VEGAS SANDS CORP as a 0.87 resolved alias, just under the 0.88 lookup gate, so the next filing under that spelling opened a second node instead of landing here. The addresses prove they are one filer rather than merely suggesting it. The loser's $17,064,000 row of 2021-06-28 was filed from 3355 LAS VEGAS BLVD. SOUTH, LAS VEGAS, NV 89109, which is the same street address the survivor's $10,000,000 row of 2021-10-21 and its five LAS VEGAS SANDS CO. rows all carry. Only one row sits elsewhere, $50,000 in 2026 from 5420 S Durango Drive, the address the company files from now. Together they gave $73,473,715 to Florida Voters in Charge, the 2022 casino amendment petition committee, against the $56,409,715 the larger node showed alone — 97 percent of everything that committee raised, from one company, which is the whole point of keeping them together. The occupations the two file under are GAMING AND ENTERTAINMENT and CASINO & RESORT COMPANY, which describe the same business. No money passes between them, so the merge makes no self-loop, and no recipient was paid the same amount under both spellings within 45 days, so nothing is double-counted. Keeping the larger node, which also holds the headquarters address."}
{"op": "merge", "keep": {"id": "34dadace-d621-4788-be5c-334e03d9903f", "name": "ROB HARDWICK"}, "lose": [{"id": "cb3f7787-1e97-454b-9ba9-b53b3165e26f", "name": "ROB HARDWICK CAMPAIGN (ST JOHNS COUNTY SHERIFF)"}, {"id": "c80a0c44-2d61-4059-a051-3d84e0824e69", "name": "ROBERT HARDWICK CAMPAIGN FUND"}], "date": "2026-09-09", "note": "Rob Hardwick's St. Johns County sheriff campaign, under the three names its filers used for it. The keeper carries 1,550 contributions worth $856,072 from 1,463 separate donors and 225 expenditures worth $849,165, the largest of them to Multi Media Services ($318,329), Data Targeting Research ($123,331), Pass Consulting Group ($113,371) and Analytics Strategy Group ($102,449). That is a campaign, not a person, which is why the node is re-kinded below. The losers are the same campaign named in full by the state feed: ROB HARDWICK CAMPAIGN (ST JOHNS COUNTY SHERIFF), $2,000 across two rows, and ROBERT HARDWICK CAMPAIGN FUND, $1,000 in and $20 out, one of whose spellings reads ROBERT A. HARDWICK CAMPAIGN FUND CRAIG. No money passes between the three, so the merge makes no self-loop. Every row on all three is dated 2019 or later; nothing here predates the 2020 race. Two look-alikes stay separate and are not this node. Rob Hardwick - Office Account is the officeholder account, a distinct account that this campaign paid $10,014 on 2021-02-01 — folding it in would turn a real transfer into a self-loop. Robert Hardwick, filed from St. Johns with the occupation Law enforcement, gave $40 to the Trump Club of St. Johns County and $500 to Jeremiah R. Blocker; that is personal giving, and attributing it to the campaign account would assert a candidate-to-candidate transfer that never happened."}
{"op": "set-kind", "entity": {"id": "34dadace-d621-4788-be5c-334e03d9903f", "name": "ROB HARDWICK"}, "kind": "candidate", "date": "2026-09-09", "note": "Ran for St. Johns County sheriff in 2020 and again in 2024. Keyed as an individual because the county feed files a candidate under a bare personal name and nothing in the row says which it is, so resolution had only the name to go on. The money says the rest: 1,463 donors in, political consultants and media buyers out. Traversable as a candidate, so the crawler can now follow the money through it instead of stopping at a leaf. The office column stays empty — there is no correction op that sets it, the same gap the four Duval candidates sit in."}
{"op": "split-rows", "from": {"id": "257cd525-8c8e-4a4e-9f6a-9a6e04a1b1e6", "name": "NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS"}, "where": {"source": "fec-fine", "raw": "NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS POLITICAL ACTION COMMITTEE"}, "to": {"name": "NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS POLITICAL ACTION COMMITTEE", "kind": "committee"}, "date": "2026-09-09", "note": "The trade association and its federal PAC are two filers, and resolution folded the second onto the first because the association's name is a prefix of the PAC's. Four rows worth $13,000, all from the FEC feed, all filed in full as NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS POLITICAL ACTION COMMITTEE. That is RPAC, the national committee, and a PAC's money is not the association's. Confirmed by the reviewer 2026-09-09. The association keeps what it filed under its own name: 52 rows as NAR - NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS, 25 as NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS and one $3,000,000 row as NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS, INC., all of them Florida filings. This is the same association-versus-PAC line drawn on the Region 7 RV committees. The new node is the national PAC and is not any of the Florida realtor committees — Realtors Political Action Committee, Realtors Political Advocacy Committee, Realtors Political Activity Committee and Realtors PAC - Florida all hold their own state account numbers at Orlando and stay separate."}
{"op": "set-kind", "entity": {"name": "TEAM FINE"}, "kind": "committee", "date": "2026-09-09", "note": "Kinded individual because the FEC files it as a bare name with no entity type on the row, and resolution reads a two-word name as a person. It is a political committee: the FEC lists it as the affiliated committee of Randy Fine for Congress, and it moved $374,599 across 13 rows into that campaign between 2025-08-29 and 2026-06-30. As a committee it is traversable, so the money can be followed through it instead of dead-ending on a phantom mega-donor."}
{"op": "set-kind", "entity": {"name": "Randy Fine for Congress"}, "kind": "committee", "date": "2026-09-09", "note": "The node existed before the FEC load, created from a single St. Johns County filing that reported receiving $5,000 from it, and a county payee with no other evidence is kinded organization. It is a federal principal campaign committee, FEC id C00893271, now carrying $3,718,554 in and $3,093,228 out. Left flagged as an injection point: the money is raised nationally and spent federally, so a trace stops here rather than treating the Florida share as a slice of that pool."}
{"op": "set-kind", "entity": {"name": "NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF REALTORS POLITICAL ACTION COMMITTEE"}, "kind": "committee", "date": "2026-09-09", "note": "Repairs the traversable flag on the node the split above created. splitEntity started every new entity terminal on the assumption that a node is promoted once it is seen receiving money, which never happens to a committee split out because it gave: this one only pays. Left as it was, the national Realtors PAC would sit in the graph as a leaf and a trace would stop at it. Both are fixed in code — splitEntity now takes traversability from the kind, and set-kind no longer returns early when the kind already matches but the flag is wrong — and this entry applies the repair to the row already created."}
{"op": "split-rows", "from": {"id": "30ee16c2-ef00-4282-b645-d463f851a814", "name": "FINE RANDY"}, "where": {"source": "fl-doe", "raw": "RANDY FINE CAMPAIGN FUND"}, "to": {"id": "cc4fe964-2e87-4e8f-ba8b-0cc5a0fecc88", "name": "Fine, Randy  (REP)(STS)"}, "date": "2026-09-10", "note": "Nineteen PAC contributions worth $16,000, filed 2023-01-23 to 2024-10-09 to RANDY FINE CAMPAIGN FUND and booked to the man rather than the campaign. The name says outright that the recipient is a campaign account, and the period is his 2024 state senate run, which is the account that was open and taking money throughout it. His state senate node already carries 14 rows filed as RANDY FINE CAMPAIGN, which is the same account under a shorter name."}
{"op": "split-rows", "from": {"id": "30ee16c2-ef00-4282-b645-d463f851a814", "name": "FINE RANDY"}, "where": {"source": "fl-doe", "raw": "FINE, RANDY"}, "to": {"id": "cc4fe964-2e87-4e8f-ba8b-0cc5a0fecc88", "name": "Fine, Randy  (REP)(STS)"}, "alias": false, "date": "2026-09-10", "note": "Eighteen more of the same, $18,000, where the PAC wrote the candidate's plain name on the check instead of the account's. Same window as the entry above, 2023-02-15 to 2024-10-22, same donors, and the same $1,000 amounts every one of them gave to the state senate account directly: Better Florida Fund, the Florida Medical Association, three Realtors committees, the Beer Wholesalers, the Fraternal Order of Police. A person does not receive PAC money; a campaign does. The spelling is deliberately not pinned as an alias — FINE, RANDY is his own name, and attaching it to a closed state senate account would send his future giving as a private person, or as a congressman, back into a 2024 campaign. The two rows that stay behind are his, $25 to his own house campaign in 2022 and $1 to his own senate campaign in 2024."}
{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/fine-randy-mirror-strays.sql", "applied": "2026-09-10", "note": "Deletes 29 rows on the VPS that the Mac no longer has. The two split-rows entries above moved 37 PAC contributions off the person FINE RANDY onto his state senate campaign; once there, 29 of them were the PACs' own filings of money the candidate had already reported, and the rebuild's mirror rule removed them as duplicates. Only 8 were new, worth $5,500, which is why the campaign went from $284,458 to $289,958 rather than to $318,458. A delta ships inserts, updates and entity tombstones, so a transaction deleted by mirror collapse has nothing to carry it: the VPS still holds all 29 pointing at the man, double-counting $28,500 his campaign account already reports. The VPS cannot repair this in its own rebuild either, because mirror collapse only pairs rows already sitting between the same two nodes and over there these are attached to an individual. Keyed on the source row hash, which both databases share, and guarded on the entity so it cannot touch a row that has since moved. Run on the VPS only; the Mac is already correct."}
{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/fec-cycle-keys.sql", "applied": "2026-09-10", "note": "Restamps 4,643 FEC rows from the made-up key fec-2026 onto 20261103-GEN, the Florida cycle for the same election. The loader had copied the 8872 namespace, which is right for an IRS quarter ending on an arbitrary date and wrong for an FEC two-year transaction period: the 2026 period runs January 2025 to December 2026 and is the same election. Keyed apart, every federal dollar fell outside the cycle filter, so Randy Fine for Congress read $0 received for 2026 against $2,768,554 unfiltered — the only row the filter could see was a $5,000 payment that reached us through a county feed. Written as an UPDATE rather than a purge and reload so the trigger stamps updated_at and the rows travel in the next delta; a reload would give them new ids against a far side that is unique on the row hash and the load would fail. Run on the Mac only. The adapter now derives the key from cycles.ts, so a fresh load needs none of this."}
