{"op": "merge", "keep": {"name": "Americans for Prosperity Action"}, "lose": [{"id": "debfe53c-dff5-4362-8c3c-f9be9c75463f", "name": "AMERICANS FOR PROPSERITY ACTION"}], "date": "2026-09-16", "note": "A sponsoring organization and the committee it funds are two entities. The resolver folded them into one because the names differ only by a corporate suffix or an ampersand. Every payment between them then became a self-loop: from_entity_id equals to_entity_id, the money counts once as given and once as received, and the edge that shows the organization funding its own committee disappears. That edge is the point where money enters the committee system, so losing it is the expensive part. Each entry below moves the rows filed under the organization's own spelling onto an entity of its own. The alias it leaves behind pins the spelling there, so the next ingest cannot fold it back. This is the misspelled twin of the federal committee: PROPSERITY for PROSPERITY. It scored below the 0.88 gate and so escaped the fold that swallowed the correct spelling, which left it as the only visible funder of the Florida committee at $162,046. One contributor, two spellings, opposite outcomes."}
{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/unfold-sponsor-selfloops.sql", "applied": "2026-09-16", "note": "`split-rows` moves from_entity_id and to_entity_id in one UPDATE, which is right for a row with the organization on one side and wrong for a self-loop. The 371 self-loops the nineteen sponsor splits were meant to divide moved whole instead, and the loop reappeared on the new entity. This puts the committee back on the endpoint that does not carry the organization's spelling. It reads entity_aliases to decide which endpoint that is, so it needs no list of spellings, and it is safe to run twice. It must be run by hand on the OVH server: corrections replay does not execute SQL."}
{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/sponsor-split-direction.sql", "applied": "2026-09-16", "note": "Companion to unfold-sponsor-selfloops.sql, and the one that settles direction. `split-rows` reads the as-filed spelling off the payer side only, so where the organization was the payee it recorded the committee's spelling instead. That left three organizations carrying their own committee's name as a manual alias, and it sent the first repair the wrong way on those pairs. This decides every endpoint from the filed names: first by comparing them to the committee's stored name, then, for the rows the state feed truncated at 40 characters, by the type tag the feed writes on a committee name and never on an organization's. Safe to run twice. It must be run by hand on the OVH server, after unfold-sponsor-selfloops.sql."}
{"op": "rename", "entity": {"id": "b3d8737d-e8d6-4533-9f23-2d6143dbb89d", "name": "Citizens for Rutherford"}, "name": "Citizens for John Rutherford", "date": "2026-09-16", "note": "The committee's registered name, taken from its FEC filings. It is John Rutherford's principal campaign committee, FEC id C00615294, and it has held that name since 2016. The node was carrying 'Citizens for Rutherford', which is how the St. Johns portal shortened it on one 2018 filing, and a county's abbreviation should not be what a federal committee is called here. The short spelling stays as an alias, so the filings that used it keep resolving."}
{"op": "set-kind", "entity": {"id": "b3d8737d-e8d6-4533-9f23-2d6143dbb89d", "name": "Citizens for John Rutherford"}, "kind": "committee", "date": "2026-09-16", "note": "Kinded organization because a county payee with no other evidence is kinded organization, and until now the only evidence here was money going out. It is a federal principal campaign committee. Kinding it committee makes it traversable, so the $49,600 it has given Duval and St. Johns filers since 2016 can be followed back into what raised it. The FEC load marks it an injection point at the same time: the money is raised nationally and spent federally, so a Florida share of that pool is not a representative slice of anything."}
{"op": "merge", "keep": {"id": "b3d8737d-e8d6-4533-9f23-2d6143dbb89d", "name": "Citizens for John Rutherford"}, "lose": [{"id": "aa67a49c-81f4-4e68-846d-b0def8a1a615", "name": "Campaign account Citizens For John Rutherford"}, {"id": "6102e661-59d7-487a-9d9a-81724ff2471e", "name": "Citizen John Rutherford Campai"}], "date": "2026-09-16", "note": "Two more spellings of the same committee, each held as its own node. 'Campaign account Citizens For John Rutherford' is one Duval filing from 2018-10-11 worth $2,500, where the filer wrote what the money came out of before writing whose account it was. 'Citizen John Rutherford Campai' is one St. Johns filing from 2026-07-29 worth $1,000, cut at 30 characters by the portal. Neither is a separate account: the committee has only ever had one, C00615294, and the FEC lists no other committee for this candidate."}
{"op": "drop-alias", "entity": {"id": "66130074-c7d4-465d-9253-06abce867d40", "name": "RUTHERFORD JOHN"}, "alias": "Citizen John Rutherford Campai", "date": "2026-09-16", "note": "The resolver had pinned the committee's truncated name to a person. It is a committee spelling and it belongs on the committee, which now holds it. Left where it was, the next county filing that arrives cut at 30 characters would send a campaign account's money to a man."}
{"op": "split-rows", "from": {"id": "f4413e73-683b-470b-916a-22eb3e650c71", "name": "CROWLEY MARITIME CORPORATION"}, "where": {"source": "fec-rutherford", "raw": "CROWLEY MARITIME CORPORATION FEDERAL PAC"}, "to": {"name": "CROWLEY MARITIME CORPORATION FEDERAL PAC", "kind": "committee"}, "date": "2026-09-16", "note": "A corporate or trade-association PAC, folded onto the corporation or association that sponsors it. Resolution matches on the normalized name, which does not strip a trailing PAC, and a sponsor's name is a prefix of its PAC's name, so the longer filed spelling scored above the auto-link threshold against the shorter node already in the graph. Forty-eight filed spellings arrived this way in the Rutherford load, worth $470,795, and forty-six of the nodes they landed on carry real Florida corporate money of their own. Left alone, each of those corporations would read as having given to a federal candidate, which no corporation may do, and the PAC that did give would not exist. This is the same line drawn on the Realtors association and its national PAC, and on the nineteen sponsors separated from their committees on 2026-09-16. 16 rows worth $39,500, filed as \"CROWLEY MARITIME CORPORATION FEDERAL PAC\" and booked to \"CROWLEY MARITIME CORPORATION\". The PAC is the filer and the money is the PAC's."}
