{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/seed-collapsed-mirror-tombstones.sql", "applied": "2026-09-17", "note": "Seeds transaction_tombstones with 2,743 rows the OVH server was never told had died. A delta carried inserts, updates and entity tombstones and had no way to carry a deleted transaction, so every row this machine deleted after having shipped it lived on over there. The server could not repair it in its own rebuild either: mirror collapse only pairs rows already sitting between the same two nodes, and a row that the correction never reached still points at the old one. Measured as the exact set difference in source_row_hash across all 315,188 expenditure rows of the two feeds that carry expenditures; nothing differed the other way and no contribution differed at all. 2,735 of them put $2,597,970 on 286 private individuals who never received it. Run on the Mac only: writing the tombstones here is what puts them in the next delta, which is what removes the rows over there. The trigger added in 0012 records every deletion from now on, so this is a one-time backlog."}
{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/stamp-county-cycle-20260917.sql", "applied": "2026-09-17", "note": "Stamps 326 county rows with 20261103-GEN. `ingest county <slug>` with no --election swept the cycle the portal opens on and then stamped nothing, because the code only derived a cycle when an election id was passed by hand. St. Johns had no null-cycle row before this and gained 109; Duval gained 217. Every figure filtered by cycle missed all of them. Both portals mark the 2026 cycle `selected`, which is 20261103-GEN. Scoped to the two feeds, to null rows and to the day they arrived, so it cannot reach Duval's 95,504 older null rows, which come from all-cycle sweeps and are a separate question. The parser now reads the `selected` option, so a later sweep needs none of this. Run on the Mac only."}
{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/drop-irs.sql", "applied": "2026-09-17", "note": "Re-run with drop-interest-and-cfr.sql: 32 IRS rows and 22 Christian Financial Resources rows had come back in the 7 and 17 September sweeps, on both databases. Both files claimed a re-sweep could not bring any of it back. It could. The SQL matched the resolved entity name, `INTERNAL REVENUE SERVICE`, while isNonPoliticalMoney matched the filed string, and St. Johns files `INTERNAL REVENUE SERVICE - IRS`. CFR was worse: the purge named it by entity id, a handle the ingest guard cannot have, so it had no rule there at all. Both gaps are now closed in isNonPoliticalMoney. The deletions were recorded by the new transaction_tombstones trigger, so unlike every earlier purge these travel to the VPS in the next delta rather than needing a hand run there."}
{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/move-forward-florida-rows.sql", "applied": "2026-09-17", "note": "Moves 6,140 rows worth $764,711 off Florida Forward (account 83794, Gainesville) and onto Forward Florida (account 65255, Fort Lauderdale), and deletes the three FORWARD FLORIDA aliases that sent them there. The two names are the same two words in opposite order, which trigram scoring rates 0.935 against the 0.88 gate, so the resolver treated one as a spelling of the other. Node 83794 held 6,302 rows and 6,140 of them, 97%, belonged to a committee it has nothing to do with; Forward Florida's own node held nothing. Matched on the name as filed rather than on the alias, because the alias is the thing that was wrong. No row has both accounts as endpoints, so the move creates no self-loop. Exact-name match now answers for Forward Florida, since its node carries that normalized name and exact match runs before alias lookup. Run on the Mac only."}
{"op": "manual-sql", "file": "migrations/manual/split-florida-forward-by-date.sql", "applied": "2026-09-17", "note": "Divides the two Florida Forward accounts by date at 2020-10-13, the first date on 83794. Account 56217 is a closed CCE in Trinity chaired by Richard Corcoran; account 83794 is an active PAC in Gainesville chaired by William S. Jones, who is also its treasurer. They are two committees and they do not merge. Both nodes carry the normalized name FLORIDA FORWARD, and the resolver's exact-match step reads WHERE normalized_name = $1 LIMIT 1 with no ORDER BY, so the same committee's rows went to one node in one run and the other node in the next: 53 expenditures on 56217 and 48 on 83794, decided by nothing but a query plan. That split also hid 33 duplicate payments from the mirror rule, each one a single transfer filed from both ends, because the rule only pairs rows already between the same two nodes. The date rule puts both halves of every pair on one node and the rebuild then collapsed 54 mirror expenditures across 39 pairs without being told about them. 8 rows stay on 56217; 225 go to 83794. Run on the Mac only."}
{"op": "merge", "keep": {"id": "a2f7bdac-d57e-4635-88a1-9cabbe065e5a", "name": "Building Florida's Future"}, "lose": [{"id": "28757194-7338-403b-b09e-559bbcc14f47", "name": "Building Florida's Future"}], "date": "2026-09-17", "note": "Accounts 55421 (closed CCE, Tallahassee) and 60948 (active PAC, Tallahassee). 25 duplicate payments worth $1,102,530. One operation registered twice, not two committees. Both accounts name William S. Jones as chair AND as treasurer, which is the test that told Florida Forward apart (Richard Corcoran's account against Stafford Jones's). Florida abolished Committees of Continuous Existence in 2013 and the operations behind them re-registered as political committees, so a closed ECO or CCE beside an active PAC with the same officers is one operation across two registrations \u2014 the JaxBiz shape mergeEntities already documents. The rows were split between the two nodes by nothing but the resolver's exact-match step, which reads WHERE normalized_name = $1 LIMIT 1 with no ORDER BY. That split hid duplicate payments from the mirror rule, which only pairs rows already sitting between the same two nodes: every overlapping (recipient, date, amount) here is one expenditure plus one contribution, never two expenditures. Merging puts both halves on one node and the rebuild collapses them."}
